# Split distinct game parts into files

When you start a **totally different part** of a game (new screen/area, new mechanic, new system, big feature that is mostly its own thing):

1. **Same game folder** — do not make a new game folder or subfolder.
2. **New file** — create another file in that folder with a clear name (e.g. `garden.js`, `cashier.js`, `enemies.js`).
3. **Wire from `index.html`** — add `<script src="that-file.js"></script>` (or `<link>` for CSS). Order matters if files depend on each other.
4. **Repeat every time** — each new distinct part gets its own file. Do not pile unrelated systems back into one giant file.

## What stays in `index.html`

- HTML shell and layout
- Shared CSS (or a shared `style.css` if you already split styles)
- Glue: loading scripts, shared globals, wiring parts together
- Plan comment at the top (update as ideas change)

## When to split vs keep together

| Split into a new file | Keep in the current file |
|-----------------------|--------------------------|
| New screen or area | Small tweak to existing part |
| New game mode or mechanic | Shared helper used everywhere |
| Big standalone system | Bug fix in code already there |

## Examples

```
Restaurant Rush/
  index.html      ← shell + loads the parts
  garden.js       ← garden screen
  kitchen.js      ← kitchen screens
  cashier.js      ← cashier / money
```

If `garden.js` later needs a **totally different** mini-game inside it, add `garden-minigame.js` in the same folder and load it after `garden.js`.
